UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Week 2, Lesson 2: Creating a character', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Creating a character for narrative means giving a concrete want, showing traits through action and speech more than adjective lists, and repeating a small consistent detail so the character feels continuous across scenes.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nInvent a character with one want and show it in three sentences without using the adjectives brave or nice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Want clear; actions/speech carry trait; no empty label pile.\n\nCHECK\nChanging personality randomly between paragraphs without story cause.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nWrite a 150-word character introduction scene with want, two shown traits and one repeatable detail. Highlight the detail and state the want in a margin note.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Creating a character for narrative means giving a concrete want, showing traits through action and speech more than adjective lists, and repeating a small consistent detail so the character feels continuous across scenes.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Writing","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN4-ECA-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Write a 150-word character introduction scene with want, two shown traits and one repeatable detail. Highlight the detail and state the want in a margin note. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using characterisation on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Changing personality randomly between paragraphs without story cause.. Correction: Strong answer: Want clear;. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Changing personality randomly between paragraphs without story cause. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating characterisation on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with characterisation, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about characterisation with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for characterisation. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for characterisation. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","Want clear; actions/speech carry trait; no empty label pile","Changing personality randomly between paragraphs without story cause","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material"],"question":"Invent a character with one want and show it in three sentences without using the adjectives brave or nice. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for characterisation; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Changing personality randomly between paragraphs without story cause","Creating a character for narrative means giving a concrete want, showing traits through action and speech more than","Want clear; actions/speech carry trait; no empty label pile","Careful use of want and explain why it matters with clear evidence"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with characterisation?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named want and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Changing personality randomly between paragraphs without story cause","Name want and explain why it matters only, without linking it to characterisation","Apply characterisation to new material and name the want and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence"],"question":"Which response best applies characterisation with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries characterisation. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Changing personality randomly between paragraphs without story cause","Point to exact words in the sentence that show characterisation and say what they do","Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event","Say the writing is good without naming any words"],"question":"Read: \"Under the pier, Sam found a locked tin and a dripping map.\" Which choice best uses characterisation from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill with textual evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Changing personality randomly between paragraphs without story cause","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged","Name want and explain why it matters without linking it to any words in the text","Want clear; actions/speech carry trait; no empty label pile"],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about characterisation?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged","Lengthen the answer with adjectives while keeping the same idea","Locate precise textual evidence, then explain how language choices shape meaning or effect","Guess from the title alone with no textual clue"],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to analyse want and explain why it matters?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake","Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess","Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length","Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea"],"question":"Which revision actually improves meaning for characterisation?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Accurate transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about characterisation","Retype the worked model as the final answer","Change fonts and claim the work is new","Changing personality randomly between paragraphs without story cause"],"question":"Which response shows accurate use of characterisation on fresh material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about characterisation with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating characterisation on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using characterisation on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for characterisation. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with characterisation, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Changing personality randomly between paragraphs without story cause.. Correction: Strong answer: Want clear;. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s4-eng-y7-w02-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise characterisation.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use characterisation with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify want and explain why it matters.","I can practise trait via action independently on a new prompt.","I can notice consistent detail needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Creating a character for narrative means giving a concrete want, showing traits through action and speech more than adjective lists, and repeating a small consistent detail so the character feels continuous across scenes.","options":["Creating a character for narrative means giving a concrete want, showing traits through action and speech more than","A claim unrelated to the text","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no explanation"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about characterisation with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nList: ''brave, funny, tall'' is thin. Shown: ''Leah laughed once, then stepped between her little brother and the slamming gate, fingers white on the strap of her bag.'' Want: protect sibling; trait: protective resolve; detail: white-knuckled strap can return later.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInvent a character with one want and show it in three sentences without using the adjectives brave or nice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Want clear; actions/speech carry trait; no empty label pile.","explain":"Creating a character for narrative means giving a concrete want, showing traits through action and speech more than adjective lists, and repeating a small consistent detail so the character feels continuous across scenes.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing personality randomly between paragraphs without story cause. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Characterisation.","caption":"Action over labels.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" wi